    "summary": "The run built a powerful but increasingly crowded Ironclad deck and handled the Act 1 boss successfully, but Act 2 combat steadily eroded HP until the Collector fight became extremely fragile. The decisive failure was not a single obviously illegal combat choice; it was entering the final fight with only 42 HP after several Act 2 fights and then failing to stabilize while facing repeated attacks. Key opportunities were also repeatedly traded for relics, requiring the Sapphire Key to be taken later under more pressure.",
    "lessons": [
      {
        "category": "COMBAT",
        "title": "At single-digit HP, prioritize survival over incremental damage",
        "situation": "When HP is already in the single digits and multiple enemies show attack intents, especially in a boss fight.",
        "lesson": "Use the strongest available defensive sequence and only attack when it removes an immediate threat or is necessary to prevent a worse state. Do not spend the remaining energy on damage by default when the next enemy attacks may be lethal.",
        "evidence_points": [
          "On Turn 7 of the Collector fight, HP was 46 with two Torch Heads and the Collector all showing ATTACK; after playing two Defend+ cards and Wild Strike, the next logged state was HP 5.",
          "At HP 5 on Turn 8, Power Through was legal and was chosen before Thunderclap, showing that the fight required emergency stabilization rather than simply maximizing damage.",
          "At HP 7 on Turn 9, both a Torch Head and the Collector were attacking, yet after Shrug It Off the remaining energy was spent on Perfected Strike rather than another defensive or fight-ending line."
        ],
        "reasoning": "The exact incoming damage is not logged, so the precise counterfactual is uncertain, but the sequential states show that the combat transitioned from 46 HP to 5 HP while several attack intents remained. This supports a reusable low-HP rule: first secure survival or remove the most dangerous attacker, then deal damage.",
        "confidence": "high"
      },
      {
        "category": "CARD_REWARD",
        "title": "Become more selective with late Act 2 card rewards",
        "situation": "When HP is low and the deck already has substantial size and several functional attacks, skills, and defensive cards.",
        "lesson": "Prefer skipping marginal rewards unless they immediately solve the current fight problem, improve draw/energy, or provide essential scaling. A larger deck is especially costly when low HP leaves little room to recover through future combats.",
        "evidence_points": [
          "The final build contained 29 cards, including five Bites, two Power Through, two Pommel Strike, and a large collection of attacks and defensive skills.",
          "At HP 19 on Act 2 Floor 29, Intimidate was added; at HP 18 on Floor 30, Thunderclap+ was added immediately before the final event and rest.",
